  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "How to read this diagram",
      "items": [
        "A sequence: time flows top to bottom. Each column is a part; each arrow is a call or a reply.",
        "It follows ONE turn end to end. A returning arrow is a response coming back.",
        "Click a column's SRC badge to see the exact code."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Glossary (plain words)",
      "items": [
        "Turn - one caller utterance and the agent's spoken reply.",
        "Slot filling - collecting the fields a booking needs (date, time, party size, name).",
        "NLU - natural-language understanding: extract the intent and the slots from the text.",
        "Barge-in - the caller interrupts while the agent is speaking, so it stops and listens."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Guided tour - one turn",
      "items": [
        "1. The caller sends voice or text.",
        "2. The pipeline transcribes it (STT).",
        "3. The dialogue manager fills slots via NLU and the booking store, and decides the reply.",
        "4. TTS speaks the reply back to the caller."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Why it is shaped this way",
      "items": [
        "The three stages are swappable and separately testable; stubs run with no models or network.",
        "The dialogue manager owns booking state, so the model is never trusted to track it.",
        "Returns are explicit so latency of each stage is visible in the trace."
      ]
    }
  ]
}